[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 457,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 370,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61781,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 42,
        "headingId": 370,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Borderline Brenner tumour is a tumour of transitional/urothelial epithelium displaying papillary architecture and lacking stromal invasion.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:54:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 458,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 371,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61782,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 42,
        "headingId": 371,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>9000/1 Brenner tumour, borderline malignancy</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:54:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 459,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 372,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61783,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 42,
        "headingId": 372,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2C73.Y &amp; XH2CH8 Other specified malignant neoplasms of the ovary &amp; Brenner tumour, borderline malignancy</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:54:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 460,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 373,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61784,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 42,
        "headingId": 373,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> atypical proliferative Brenner tumour</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:54:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 461,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 374,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61785,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 42,
        "headingId": 374,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:54:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 462,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61786,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 42,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Tumours are unilateral and confined to the ovary {22954784}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:54:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61787,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 42,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Patients often present with a pelvic mass {22954784}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:54:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61788,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 42,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>This is a very uncommon tumour and patients are usually &gt;&nbsp;50&nbsp;years old {22954784}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:54:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61789,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 42,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These tumours are presumed to arise from benign Brenner tumours.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:54:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61790,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 42,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "<p>Disease-specific mutations are not known. Alterations in <em>CDKN2A</em> (the gene encoding p16INK4a) have been reported, resulting in loss of p16 staining by immunohistochemistry {23887305}. Mutations may be present in <em>KRAS</em> and <em>PIK3CA</em> {23887305}, but not in <em>TP53</em> {28639280; 19033864}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-02T17:52:52"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61791,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 42,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unlike benign Brenner tumours, borderline Brenner tumours are typically large (median size: 12&nbsp;cm) and cystic {22954784}. Papillary masses project into the cyst lumen. Solid areas often reflect a benign Brenner component. Rarely, the tumour is completely solid.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:54:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61792,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 42,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The tumours resemble low-grade papillary urothelial neoplasms of the urothelial tract. The cytological features can vary, but the cells are usually uniform and show elongated nuclei with fine chromatin and visible nucleoli; occasionally, a moderate to severe degree of cytological atypia is present. Mucinous or squamous metaplasia is often found. Areas of benign Brenner tumour are nearly always present. An uncommon pattern is one with marked crowding of the nests of transitional epithelium, which may be large and tortuous. Although there is increased mitotic activity and/or cytological atypia in these lesions, there should be no evidence of stromal invasion. Tumours with stromal invasion, as defined by infiltrative nests with a desmoplastic stromal response, should be designated as malignant Brenner tumours.</p>\r\n<p><em>Immunohistochemistry</em></p>\r\n<p>Immunohistochemistry is usually positive for p63 and GATA3 and negative for ER, PR, and WT1 {19033864; 19092634; 17880529; 14576476}. p53 shows a wildtype immunoreactive pattern, and p16 can show loss of staining {23887305}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:54:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61793,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 42,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:54:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61794,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 42,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:54:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61795,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 42,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> papillary growth resembling low-grade papillary urothelial neoplasms of the urothelial tract or (rarely) crowded nests of transitional/urothelial epithelium, without stromal invasion.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> a benign Brenner component.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:54:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61796,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 42,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "<p>Borderline Brenner tumour is staged according to the Union for International Cancer Control (UICC) TNM classification (see <em>TNM staging of ovarian, fallopian tube, and primary peritoneal carcinoma</em>&nbsp;[[Brierley JD, Gospodarowicz MK, Wittekind C, editors. TNM classification of malignant tumours. 8th ed. Oxford (UK): Wiley-Blackwell; 2017.]]) and the FIGO staging system.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-15T11:08:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61797,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 42,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The behaviour is usually benign; local recurrence is rare {22954784; 5074634}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:54:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]